(a) Definitions. The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this section shall have the meanings ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Business means any person, proprietorship, partnership, individual firm or entity conducting commerce, production, or procurement.
Fulltime equivalent employee means an individual who works 40 hours or more per week and receives a "W-2" form from an employer. Each fulltime employee equals one fulltime equivalent employee. Parttime employees are calculated as a fulltime position equivalent basis by adding together the average weekly hours of employees who work less than 40 hours weekly, taking that sum and dividing it by 40.
Occupational tax means a tax on all persons, proprietors, partnerships, firms, and corporations engaging in or offering to engage in any trade, business, calling, avocation or profession from a permanent location in the city.
(b) Business transactions within the city limits. Each and every person, firm or corporation transacting or offering to transact any business or working at or offering to work at any trade, occupation or profession within the limits of the city (except such as are exempt by law) shall pay occupational taxes annually to the city clerk, after approval by the city manager in certain instances hereinafter provided for, in accordance with further provisions of this chapter.
(1) Taxes due. Taxes are due on or before January 15 of each year. For new businesses, taxes are due prior to the commencement of any business activity. In the event that any person commences business on any date after the date specified in this section, the tax shall be due and payable 30 days following the commencement of the business
(2) Tax rates.
a. At the time when occupational taxes are due, the applicant shall pay to the clerk and treasurer of the city an amount as hereinafter provided set by ordinance or resolution of the mayor and council, and made available for public examination in the office of the city clerk. In November of each year, the mayor and council in regular meeting, shall set tax rates based upon the number of employees working in the city for each business, trade, occupation or profession to take effect the succeeding year, subject to review by the incoming mayor and council at any regular meeting held in January of the succeeding year, in those years in which an election for a mayor and council is held. Once the occupational tax rate has been set by the mayor and council in accordance with the provisions of this section, the same shall remain in effect for future years until changed by a valid action thereon by the mayor and council under the provisions of this section.
b. Rate schedule.
1. Before any person shall locate, relocate or operate any business within the limits of the city, such person shall first pay occupational taxes to the city based upon a rate schedule set by ordinance or resolution of the mayor and council, and made available for public examination in the office of the city clerk.
2. Exemptions. The following list of occupations are required by state law to either pay a flat rate occupational tax not to exceed $400.00 per state certificate holder to the city or a local occupational tax assessment based on the local government's rate schedule. In the city, such business as listed below may either pay a flat tax of $400.00 per state certificate holder annually or follow the rate schedule as established by ordinance or resolution of the mayor and council, and made available for public examination in the office of the city clerk.
(i) Lawyers;
(ii) Physician licensed under chapter 34 of Title 43 (O.C.G.A. § 43-34-20 et seq.);
(iii) Osteopath licensed under chapter 34 of Title 43 (O.C.G.A. § 43-34-20 et seq.);
(iv) Chiropractors;
(v) Podiatrists;
(vi) Dentists;
(vii) Optometrists;
(viii) Psychologists;
(ix) Veterinarians;
(x) Landscape architects;
(xi) Land surveyors;
(xii) Practitioners of physiotherapy;
(xiii) Certified Public Accountants;
(xiv) Embalmers;
(xv) Funeral directors (this does not mean funeral homes);
(xvi) Civil; mechanical; hydraulic; or electrical engineers;
(xvii) Architects;
(xviii) Marriage and family therapists; social workers; and professional counselors;
(xix) Dealers of motor vehicles as defined in paragraph (1) of section 10-1-622 (O.C.G.A. § 10-1-622(1)); and
(xx) Any other business, profession, or occupation for which state licensure or registration is required by state law, unless the state law regulating such business, profession, or occupation specifically allows for regulation by local governments.
(3) Conformance with appendix A, pertaining to zoning. All businesses operating within the city limits must adhere to regulations established in appendix A, pertaining to zoning. No taxes may be paid for a business which does not comply with the zoning regulations.
(4) Taxes to be paid before activity commences. It shall be unlawful for any person, firm or corporation to transact any business or work at or offer to work at any business, trade, occupation or profession within the city limits (except those exempt by law) without first having paid occupational taxes.
(5) Enforcement. If any person, firm or corporation embraced in the forgoing sections shall fail or refuse to pay occupational taxes therein required of them the year for which the same is required within the time required, he, she or they shall be notified by the city manager or code enforcement official to take out and pay said taxes, and upon failure to comply, they shall be guilty of a breach of this section, and upon conviction before the city court shall be required to at once take out and pay said tax, and shall be punished according to the general penalties described in section 1-8. For all said sums the clerk shall issue an execution against said delinquent and against his property and place same in the hands of the chief of police for levy and sale under the same rules as given the levy and sale under tax executions.
(6) Jurisdiction. All businesses that have a physical establishment based, owned, leased, or rented in the city limits shall be required to pay an occupational tax proportional to the number of employees, both fulltime and parttime, working at or in combination with such establishment.
(7) Appeal. A person that has any business operating within the city limits that feels his occupational tax assessment misrepresents the impact and burden of their business operation on the infrastructure and services provided by the city may be heard before the mayor and council at a regularly scheduled meeting prior to the date for which taxes are due, which is listed in this section.
(Code 1998, § 17-207)

